    // index
    "ix.0": "High School · US History Since 1877 · §113.41 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "Birmingham, 1963 — the Civil Rights Movement",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into the spring of 1963 in Birmingham, take on a stakeholder's point of view, and work a real, ill-structured question about unjust law, nonviolence, and change — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> It is the spring of 1963 in Birmingham. When the law itself is unjust, how should the movement push for change — and how should students, leaders, and officials respond?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Vocabulary, key facts about segregation and the movement, a jigsaw read, and a timeline of the road to Birmingham. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect &amp; organize",
    "ix.9": "Strategies–consequences concept map, primary sources, competing points of view, and a structured argument. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
    "ix.11": "Meet the problem, take a stakeholder role, investigate, propose &amp; defend a course of action, debrief. ~2–4 periods.",
    "ix.12": "⛔ <b>Gate:</b> don't open the Phase 3 problem until students have finished the surface and deep activities. Meeting the problem too early turns inquiry into guessing.",
    "ix.13": "Big idea &amp; objectives",
    "ix.14": "<b>Big idea:</b> When people conclude that the law itself is unjust, they must weigh <b>strategy, risk, and conscience</b> in deciding how to press for change — and the choices made in Birmingham in 1963 moved a nation and shaped the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Voting Rights Act of 1965.",
    "ix.15": "<b>Know:</b> the development of the civil rights movement — Brown v. Board, the Montgomery Bus Boycott, the sit-ins and SNCC, the Birmingham Campaign and Children's Crusade, the March on Washington, and the landmark laws that resulted.",
    "ix.16": "<b>Understand:</b> the same spring of 1963 looks profoundly different from each stakeholder's point of view — leader, student, moderate, official, and federal government alike.",
    "ix.17": "<b>Do:</b> run the problem-solving and decision-making process end to end — identify the problem, gather information, list and weigh options, choose and defend a course of action, and evaluate it (§113.41(c)(31)(B)).",
    "ix.18": "Standards this unit is aligned to (§113.41)",
    "ix.19": "TEKS SE",
    "ix.20": "Where it lives in the unit",
    "ix.21": "The impact of the civil rights movement — its development, key events, leaders, and documents — Surface facts, Deep sources",
    "ix.22": "Analyze and evaluate information; identify point of view and bias in sources — Deep analysis, Transfer investigation",
    "ix.23": "Communicate findings in written and oral form — Deep argument, Transfer brief",
    "ix.24": "<b>The problem-solving &amp; decision-making process — the entire Transfer phase</b>",
    "ix.25": "Teacher prep &amp; materials",
    "ix.26": "Print the <a href=\"facilitator-guide.html\">facilitator guide</a> (teacher moves, map of possibilities, role cards, debrief prompts) and the <a href=\"assessment.html\">assessment pack</a> (rubric + individual-in-group).",
    "ix.27": "Chart paper or a projected space for three shared columns: <b>Hunches · Know · Need-to-Know</b> (the KWHL chart).",
